{"qid":"Q813","names":["Kyrgyzstan"],"title":"Kyrgyzstan","endonym":{"native":"Кыргызстан","lang":"ky"},"capsule":"The Kyrgyz Republic declared independence from the dissolving Soviet Union on 31 August 1991 and adopted its current constitution in 1993; the from-date of 1992 reflects international recognition. Askar Akayev governed as the first president until the Tulip Revolution of March 2005 ousted him; a second political upheaval in April 2010 removed Kurmanbek Bakiyev, and subsequent constitutional reforms in 2010 and 2021 reconfigured executive power. Kyrgyzstan is a largely mountainous, landlocked country in Central Asia dominated by the Tian Shan range; it joined the Eurasian Economic Union in 2015.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Kyrgyzstan's internationally recognized borders are well defined, though the southern border with Tajikistan contains disputed sections that remain incompletely demarcated.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Kyrgyzstan","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kyrgyzstan"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kyrgyzstan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyrgyzstan"},{"label":"Wikipedia: History of Kyrgyzstan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Kyrgyzstan"}],"britannica":"place/Kyrgyzstan","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kyrgyzstan","rulers":[{"qid":"Q502083","name":"Andrei Iordan","start":1991,"end":1992,"image":null},{"qid":"Q24693","name":"Tursunbek Chyngyshev","start":1992,"end":1993,"image":"Турсунбек Чынгышев 1993-1.png","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%D0%A2%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%BA%20%D0%A7%D1%8B%D